{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Claim\n\nThe core reconciler passes its transport around as an UNANNOTATED parameter. That is why the Backend\nport can silently under-declare what the core requires — the defect class behind\n[rebar:a357-b747-ece9-4cf5], where a DC writing reconcile pass crashed on a method\n(`set_entity_property`) that the core calls from three sites and `TicketTransport` never declared.\n\nThis is not an adjacent tidiness issue. It is the ENABLING CONDITION.\n\n## Proof — three-way mypy probe (RE-VERIFIED, and the enabling detail it omitted)\n\n    def current_shape(client, ...):                     # today's core\n        client.set_entity_property(...)                 # -> NO ERROR\n\n    def annotated_any(client: Any, ...):                # what the ratchet alone accepts\n        client.set_entity_property(...)                 # -> NO ERROR\n\n    def annotated_port(client: TicketTransport, ...):   # typed as the PORT\n        client.definitely_not_a_member(...)\n        # -> error: \"TicketTransport\" has no attribute \"definitely_not_a_member\"  [attr-defined]\n\nRun with the repo's own settings — `mypy src/rebar`, i.e. exactly what `make typecheck` runs.\n\n**The third row only holds when mypy can RESOLVE the annotation, and inside this package that is\nnot automatic.** Every core reconciler module imports its siblings absolutely as\n`from rebar_reconciler.X import …` (87 such imports), because the package is executed as the\ntop-level name `rebar_reconciler` with `src/rebar/_engine` injected on `sys.path`. mypy is never\ntold about that injection, `rebar_reconciler` is not an importable distribution, and\n`ignore_missing_imports = true` is set — so **`from rebar_reconciler._backend import TicketTransport`\nresolves to `Any`**, and a parameter annotated with it is exactly as blind as `client: Any`.\nMeasured with `reveal_type`, both forms in one file:\n\n    from rebar_reconciler._backend import TicketTransport as TT_shim\n    from rebar._engine.rebar_reconciler._backend import TicketTransport as TT_real\n    def f(a: TT_shim, b: TT_real) -> None:\n        reveal_type(a)   # -> \"Any\"                    <- silently unchecked\n        reveal_type(b)   # -> \"rebar._engine.rebar_reconciler._backend.TicketTransport\"\n\nA **relative** import inside a reconciler module resolves correctly and restores the check:\n\n    # src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/<any module>.py\n    from ._backend import TicketTransport\n    def f(a: TicketTransport) -> None:\n        a.definitely_not_a_member(\"x\")       # -> error: ... has no attribute ...  [attr-defined]\n        a.set_entity_property(\"K-1\", \"p\", 1) # -> no error\n\nSo the requirement is sharper than the original plan stated: **annotate as the port AND import the\nport by a path mypy resolves.** Getting only the first half ships annotations that look rigorous and\nenforce nothing — the exact failure this ticket exists to prevent, arriving by a different route.\n\n## The metaclass is NOT the obstacle (a prior blocking finding on this ticket, now refuted)\n\nAn earlier investigation recorded that\n`class TicketTransport(Protocol, metaclass=_TransportPortMeta)` (`_backend.py:195`, with\n`# type: ignore[misc]` on the metaclass at `:138`) makes mypy stop attribute-checking the annotated\ntype, and concluded the class had to be split before this story could proceed. **That measurement\nwas confounded by the import path**, not by the metaclass: its probe reached `TicketTransport`\nthrough the unresolvable `rebar_reconciler` shim (→ `Any`) and compared it against a Protocol\ndeclared locally in the same file (→ resolved). Re-run with the import path held constant, mypy\nattribute-checks the metaclass-bearing Protocol normally — the `[attr-defined]` error above is\nagainst today's `TicketTransport`, metaclass and all. Composition also survives: a\n`class X(TicketTransport, SupportsComments, Protocol)` view is likewise attribute-checked.\n\n`_TransportPortMeta` therefore stays exactly as it is, and this story does not touch it. It is\nload-bearing for the RUNTIME side and independently verified as such: `isinstance` against a plain\n`@runtime_checkable` Protocol goes through `ABCMeta`'s subclass cache and keeps answering `True`\nafter a member is deleted (measured), whereas `isinstance(x, TicketTransport)` correctly flips to\n`False`. Note also that `assert_transport_conforms()` ([rebar:bda8-370d-23ad-4e36]) is `hasattr`-based\nand never calls `isinstance`, so it is immune to the cache by construction; the metaclass is what\nkeeps the *`isinstance` spelling* honest for the held-out conformance tests.\n\n## The important subtlety — the EXISTING ratchet does not fix this\n\n`pyproject.toml` documents a shrink-only mypy strictness ratchet (per-package\n`disallow_untyped_defs`, enforced by `test_mypy_ratchet.py`), currently enabling\n`rebar.graph.*` and `rebar.grounding.*`, with `_engine` in the exempt set awaiting promotion.\n\n**Promoting `_engine` through that ratchet would NOT have prevented a357** — see the middle row\nabove: `client: Any` satisfies `disallow_untyped_defs` and remains completely blind. The requirement\nis not 'annotate the params', it is 'annotate them AS THE PORT, resolvably'. A promotion done the\neasy way (spraying `Any`) — or done with a port annotation imported through the `rebar_reconciler`\nshim, which mypy widens to `Any` for you — would close the ratchet ticket while leaving this defect\nfully intact. That is the outcome to guard against, and it now has two routes, not one.\n\n## Scale (measured)\n\n15 functions in `rebar_reconciler` take a `client`/`transport` parameter with NO annotation;\n10 already annotate one. Sample untyped: `dispatch_one.create_one`, `dispatch_one.update_one`,\n`dispatch_apply_phases._update_one_apply_reporter`, `batch_dispatch.delete_one`,\n`apply_inbound.inbound_repair_property`. Any existing annotation that names a port type through the\n`rebar_reconciler.` shim path counts as untyped for this story's purpose and must be re-pointed.\n\n**Sizing probe, run:** annotating `client` on `dispatch_one.create_one` and `update_one` with a\nrelatively-imported `TicketTransport` produced exactly ONE new mypy error — `\"TicketTransport\" has\nno attribute \"add_comment\"` at `dispatch_one.py:461`. That single finding is informative out of\nproportion to its size; see the next section.\n\n## The finding the sizing probe surfaced — capability members\n\n`add_comment` is one of the four OPT-IN capability members (`set_relationship`,\n`get_issuelinks_map`, `add_comment`, `get_comment_map`) that `_TransportPortMeta` carries as\nmetaclass attributes rather than class-body members, deliberately: `hasattr(TicketTransport,\n\"add_comment\")` is `True` so the AST audit can attribute them, while `typing`'s protocol-attribute\ncollection never sees them, so a links-less or comment-less transport still conforms.\n\nmypy does not consult metaclass attributes for a Protocol-typed value, so it reports these sites —\nand it is RIGHT to. The existing AST audit\n(`test_every_transport_member_the_core_reaches_for_is_declared`) cannot report them, because\n`hasattr` says `True`. **mypy is strictly stronger than the audit here**, and this is the first\nconcrete demonstration of that.\n\nThe correct resolution at such a site is to NARROW the annotation to a composed view that names the\ncapability the site actually requires — verified to type-check:\n\n    class _CommentingTransport(TicketTransport, SupportsComments, Protocol): ...\n\nIt is NOT to add `add_comment` to the `TicketTransport` class body (that would force every future\ntransport to provide a feature it was never obliged to have), and NOT to widen back to `Any`.\n\n## Interaction with J9 [rebar:bda8-370d-23ad-4e36], settled\n\nJ9 already landed `assert_transport_conforms()`, a RUNTIME conformance guard wired into both backend\nfactories, plus the AST audit. This story is their STATIC complement, not their replacement. All\nthree stay, and they catch different things:\n\n* runtime guard — a transport ASSEMBLED without a required member, caught at construction, before\n  any write.\n* AST audit — a core module reaching for a member no port declares, caught for the always-required\n  set.\n* mypy — a core CALL SITE reaching for a member its annotated view does not offer, caught at\n  authoring time, including the capability members the audit structurally cannot see.\n\nRemoving or weakening any of them is out of scope.\n\n## Also explains [rebar:dbf9-3fc8-625d-4730]\n\nThe same `Any` is why Serena/Pyright returns a FALSE EMPTY for\n`find_referencing_symbols('set_entity_property')` — no resolvable receiver type, so no references.\nNote this cuts the same way as the mypy finding: the unresolvable `rebar_reconciler.` import is a\nsecond, independent source of that blindness, so navigation only recovers where BOTH the annotation\nand its import path are fixed.\n\n## Approach\n\n**Annotate as the PORT, import the PORT resolvably, then let mypy find the gaps.**\n\n1. **Re-point the port import** in each core module that will carry an annotation: replace\n   `from rebar_reconciler._backend import …` with `from ._backend import …` for the types used in\n   annotations. Relative imports are already used in this package\n   (`adapters/__init__.py`) and are correct at runtime under both module names, so this is not a\n   runtime behaviour change. A `TYPE_CHECKING`-guarded relative import is an acceptable variant\n   where a module has a reason to keep its runtime import absolute.\n2. **Annotate** each unannotated `client`/`transport` parameter with `TicketTransport`, or with a\n   composed/capability Protocol where the site genuinely needs one.\n3. **Probe before editing.** Run the annotation pass and record the resulting `[attr-defined]` list\n   on this ticket BEFORE resolving it, so the change's size is known rather than discovered halfway.\n   Expected shape, from the sizing probe: mostly capability members, a small tail of genuine port\n   gaps.\n4. **Treat every `[attr-defined]` as a FINDING, not noise.** Resolve each by either DECLARING the\n   member on `TicketTransport` (the core genuinely always requires it) or NARROWING the annotation\n   to a composed capability view (the member is opt-in). Never by widening back to `Any`, and never\n   by reverting to the shim import path — those are the two ways this change can silently\n   self-neutralise.\n\n**Do NOT promote `_engine` through the strictness ratchet in this story.** The ratchet enables\n`disallow_untyped_defs`, which `client: Any` satisfies while remaining completely blind. Promotion\nand port-typing are separate changes and conflating them is how the easy version ships and closes\nboth.\n\n## Scope\n\n- `src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/**` — re-point port imports to a mypy-resolvable path and\n  annotate the `client`/`transport` parameters.\n- `src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/_backend.py` — declare any member mypy proves the core\n  ALWAYS requires and the port omits; add composed capability views where sites need them. The\n  `_TransportPortMeta` metaclass and `assert_transport_conforms()` are NOT modified.\n- tests pinning that neither self-neutralising route passes (see Testing).\n- a note in `pyproject.toml` / `docs/architecture.md` per the last AC.\n\n**NOT in scope:** promoting `_engine` through the mypy strictness ratchet; changing\n`_TransportPortMeta`, `assert_transport_conforms()`, or the AST audit; converting the package's\nother 80-odd non-annotation imports to relative form (a separate, larger clean-up — this story\nre-points only what its annotations need); any behaviour change whatsoever.\n\n## Testing\n\n* `make typecheck` is clean at the resulting strictness, and `make lint` passes.\n* the full reconciler unit suite passes UNEDITED — this is a pure annotation + import-form change,\n  so any test that needs editing means behaviour moved and the change has failed its own premise.\n* the backend contract / conformance suites pass unchanged, demonstrating the runtime guard and the\n  AST audit are untouched.\n* **the blindness guard, which is the load-bearing test.** A check over the core reconciler that\n  fails if a `client`/`transport` parameter is annotated `Any`, left bare, OR annotated with a port\n  type imported through the unresolvable `rebar_reconciler.` path. All three are equivalent to no\n  annotation at all under `mypy src/rebar`, and the third is the one a reviewer will not see.\n* a NEGATIVE mypy probe, run and recorded: a scratch call to an undeclared member through a\n  port-typed parameter in a core module must produce `[attr-defined]`, proving the annotation\n  enforces something rather than merely being present.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] every `client`/`transport` parameter in the core reconciler is annotated with the PORT type\n      (`TicketTransport`, or a capability/composed Protocol where the site needs one), NOT `Any`\n- [ ] every such annotation names a type mypy actually RESOLVES — verified by `reveal_type`\n      returning a `rebar._engine.rebar_reconciler...` type, never `Any`, for at least one parameter\n      per touched module\n- [ ] a scratch call to an undeclared member through one of those parameters produces\n      `[attr-defined]` under `mypy src/rebar`; the transcript is recorded on this ticket\n- [ ] every `[attr-defined]` the annotation pass produced is resolved by declaring the member on\n      `TicketTransport` or by narrowing to a capability view — none by widening to `Any` and none by\n      reverting an import to the `rebar_reconciler.` path; the full list and its per-item resolution\n      is recorded on this ticket\n- [ ] a test fails if a core `client`/`transport` param is annotated `Any`, left bare, or annotated\n      through the unresolvable shim import path, so a future 'annotate it to pass the ratchet'\n      change cannot silently reintroduce the blindness\n- [ ] `_TransportPortMeta`, `assert_transport_conforms()`, and the AST audit are byte-unchanged, and\n      the held-out conformance tests still pass — the static check is added ALONGSIDE the runtime\n      guard, not in place of it\n- [ ] `mypy src/rebar` is clean, and the reconciler unit + contract suites pass unedited\n- [ ] the ratchet note in `pyproject.toml` / `docs/architecture.md` records BOTH defeat routes: that\n      annotating as `Any` satisfies `disallow_untyped_defs` while defeating its purpose at port\n      boundaries, AND that a port annotation imported via `rebar_reconciler.` is silently widened to\n      `Any` by `ignore_missing_imports`"}},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1785643631142661001,"uuid":"ba390990-bce3-421e-afd8-d24c9df683bc"}